# ============================================================================
# SEVEN-STEP WORK SESSION (plans/03-expand.md), CYCLE 1 on this repo.
# Expanded from the two-step probe, which is kept at
# scenarios/discourse.yaml.probe-2step.bak.
#
# THE MINI-BENCH THAT AUTHORISED THIS EXPANSION - both arms, x1, unscored, real wall
# (results/loop/discourse/minibench.md):
#     group        baseline (per run)   sense (per run)   delta
#     contract     3/4                  4/4               +0.25
#     dependents   6/15 = 0.400         14/15 = 0.933     +0.533   <== WIN
#   baseline 0.400 <= the 0.50 ceiling; delta +0.533 >= the +0.50 bar; both conditions hold.
#   Both arms exited 0, no watchdog, both finished with wall unspent (sense 371 of 480,
#   baseline 289 of 446). The baseline's 0.400 is where it STOPPED, not where it was cut.
#   Its route was 30 tool calls, 23 of them greps, ending in a `grep -rln "Category\b" |
#   xargs grep -c "Category\." | sort -rn` frequency census it then hand-read down - not the
#   one-grep-returns-the-set shape. The rows it never reached are the quiet single-touch app
#   files and the plugin periphery.
#   Free rows, cited by BOTH arms, which are the whole of the baseline's floor:
#     d:backup-restorer, d:email-receiver, d:tags-search, d:topic-guardian,
#     d:topic-view-serializer, d:topics-controller.
#   Cited by NEITHER arm at x1: d:migrations-optimizer. One row of fifteen; watched, not cut.
#   No superseded question: this is cycle 1, nothing was killed by a number.
#
# WHAT THE EXPANSION CHANGED, AND WHAT IT DID NOT
#   NOT changed: step 4 is the probe's step 2 byte for byte, and the `dependents` gold is the
#   same fifteen rows at the same lines. Move either and the +0.533 stops describing this
#   scenario. Step 2 is the probe's step 1, likewise unchanged.
#   Added: steps 1, 3, 5, 6, 7 around them, and eight rows in two NON-SCORING groups -
#   `write-path` (4) and `guards` (4). Nothing was added to `dependents`: gold that never
#   faced a baseline is untested gold.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO `specs` GROUP. Step 7 asks for the tests that must be updated or added, but
# the headline scores `path:line` and a test that must be ADDED has no line to cite. The
# banked Status control measured exactly this: its spec rows scored mentioned 2/2 in 8 of 10
# runs and cited 0/2 in 10 of 10, both arms, both models, since June. The step still asks for
# them - the judge's map_quality sees the answer - but they are not gold.
#
# WHERE THE ADDED ROWS CAME FROM, stated because three of them are not in the blast payload.
#   Shown in `sense_blast(symbol: "Category", file: "app/models/category.rb")`:
#     w:seed-categories, w:import-export-importer, g:data-explorer-parameter,
#     g:chat-integration-rule, g:site-setting-validation.
#   NOT in that payload, sourced by reading app/models/category.rb and following the
#   registered name: w:index-for-search-job, w:category-stats-job, g:chat-plugin-event.
#   These three are reached through `Jobs.enqueue(:index_category_for_search, ...)` and
#   `DiscourseEvent.trigger(:category_updated, ...)` - symbol-registry dispatch, which the
#   payload's own index_caveat names as a known gap ("plugin extensions
#   (DiscoursePluginRegistry, add_to_class) ... method_missing dispatch"). They are the true
#   answer to "which parts run asynchronously", they are equally reachable from the model file
#   by either arm, and they sit in non-scoring groups, so they decide nothing.
#   Every one of the eight added rows was opened and its line read by hand; not one credit
#   comes from a script tally.
#
# STEP ORDER AND LEAKAGE. Nothing before step 4 names a directory a `dependents` row sits in.
# The step 5 guard rows land in plugins/chat/plugin.rb and lib/site_settings/validations.rb -
# both AFTER the discriminator, so they cannot hand it anything - and lib/guardian/ was
# deliberately left out of every added row, because d:topic-guardian lives there.
# ============================================================================
#
# ---- carried from the probe scenario, unchanged ----------------------------
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 1 on this repo.
# Not a re-question: results/loop/discourse/cycles.jsonl does not exist, so there is no
# trajectory to correct from. `ls verticals/ruby-rails/results/loop/discourse/` returns
# author.agent.json and author.agent.log only - no measured run on this repo, either arm.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KIND OF QUESTION, AND WHY IT IS THE ONE ON THE MENU
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The strongest measured kind - "what HOLDS X, and what stays alive when X goes away" -
# is OFF THE MENU here, and that is measured, not assumed:
#     python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#     SiteSetting/User/Post/DiscourseRoute/Topic/Base/ApplicationSerializer/Array/
#     Category/InvalidParameters/Group/Guardian  ->  rows 0, files 0, vias 0, "under bar"
#     0 candidate(s) above the bar.
# Twelve for twelve at zero. That reproduces the standing law's finding on the two Ruby
# repos already swept, and it takes the retention kind off this repo too.
#
# So this is kind one, an occurrence-list question, and the plan requires the sentence
# saying what makes it the first sort and not the second. See THE AXIS below: the ask is
# not for the occurrence list. It is for the ROUTINE each occurrence sits inside.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEAM PROFILE - recorded, never a gate
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three candidates profiled. `Upload` and `Category` first returned PRECISION=0.00 with
# 0 app caller files; that is symbol ambiguity, not a resolver gap - sense_blast replied
#   {"ambiguous": true, "matches": 6, "top_matches": ["Upload (204 edges, class) app/models/upload.rb:5", ...]}
# and both profile cleanly once --file pins the model. Re-run with the file pinned:
#
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Category --file app/models/category.rb --propose
#     app caller files : 89    SCATTER (dirs) : 47
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Category' in 109 app files   PRECISION=0.82  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + grep-clean
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Upload --file app/models/upload.rb --propose
#     app caller files : 85    SCATTER (dirs) : 36
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Upload' in 80 app files      PRECISION=1.06
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Guardian --file lib/guardian.rb --propose
#     app caller files : 130   SCATTER (dirs) : 50
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Guardian' in 102 app files   PRECISION=1.27
#
# Category leads the ranking on the one figure that has separated wins from ties across
# three verticals: 0.82, the lowest of the three. PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS - the
# two-arm run in the next phase is what rejects.
#
# A KILLER RUN AGAINST MY OWN REASON FOR PREFERRING IT. I first wanted to pass over
# Category on the theory that its dependents are `category_*.rb` association models that
# `git ls-files | grep category` prints for free - which is true of its 28
# affected_via_composition rows (CategoryCustomField, CategoryGroup, CategoryTagStat...).
# Run against the whole dependent set, the theory dies:
#     Category: 89 dependent files, 16 have "category" in the PATH (18%)
#     Upload:   85 dependent files, 23 have "upload"   in the PATH (27%)
#     Guardian: 130 dependent files, 0 have "guardian" in the PATH (0%)
# 18% is the LOWEST path-transcription share of the two anchors whose token is in their
# paths at all. The theory was backwards; it is recorded here because it nearly chose the
# anchor. Every composition row is excluded from gold below for exactly this reason.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE STRUCTURAL FACT THIS DRAFT IS BUILT AGAINST - quoted, not estimated
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every one of the 60 shown `direct_callers` rows carries "relation":"calls Category", and
# plan step 3 requires every gold row to come from that shown set. So gold on this anchor
# is a subset of the token occurrences BY CONSTRUCTION and cannot be dark. Confirmed by
# opening all fifteen gold files: every pinned line writes the literal token `Category`.
# That is the same fact the mastodon cycles recorded, and it is why no darkness lever,
# no anchor move and no rewording is attempted here - all three are closed, measured, on
# a Ruby repo with this exact payload shape.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS:
#   for each dependent, the ENCLOSING ROUTINE the dependency sits inside - the method that
#   owns the line and whose behaviour actually changes. A search prints the matching line,
#   its path and its number; it never prints the definition that encloses the match.
#
# That asymmetry is real in THIS payload, not borrowed. The shown blast set names the
# routine on every row, free:
#     app/services/user_destroyer.rb:16   | UserDestroyer#destroy
#     app/services/search_indexer.rb:269  | SearchIndexer.index
#     lib/email/receiver.rb:845           | Email::Receiver#sent_to_mailinglist_mirror?
# and `grep -n '\bCategory\b'` on those same three files gives:
#     app/services/user_destroyer.rb:37:      category_topic_ids = Category.where.not(topic_id: nil).pluck(:topic_id)
#     app/services/search_indexer.rb:345:    if Category === obj && (obj.saved_change_to_name? || force)
#     lib/email/receiver.rb:849:            return true if destination.is_a?(Category) && destination.mailinglist_mirror?
# Not one of the three grep lines contains the name of the routine it is inside.
#
# THE MECHANISM SENTENCE, which the next phase measures: a dependent reaches the category
# class once, in the middle of doing some other job - destroying a user, indexing for
# search, deciding whether an inbound email is a mailing-list mirror - from inside a
# routine whose own name is about that other job and never about categories, so the line
# that takes the dependency and the routine that owns it are about two different subjects,
# and the line alone does not say whose behaviour changes when the contract moves.
#
# GOLD IS CURATED FOR THAT AXIS. Every one of the fifteen `dependents` rows was opened and
# its enclosing routine read by hand. Rows whose routine is named after categories
# (Jobs::ReindexSearch#rebuild_categories, ImportExport::Importer#import_categories,
# SiteSettings::Validations#validate_category_ids, PostRevisionSerializer#filter_category_id,
# Tags::Search#fetch_category, PostRevision#categories) were REJECTED even though they are
# in the shown payload: on those the routine name restates the line and the axis does not
# bite. Two rows were also rejected for sitting in a class body rather than a method, where
# the payload names no routine either (lib/search.rb, lib/post_revisor.rb - the shown rows
# are `Search` at :3 and `PostRevisor` at :6).
#
# STEP 1 CANNOT BE CASHED IN STEP 2. Step 1 orients on ONE narrow sub-surface: resolving a
# category when all the system holds is a slug path from a URL. Its files are the
# non-scoring `contract` group. An earlier draft used the permission surface and was
# changed: it puts CategoryGuardian in step 1 while gold row d:topic-guardian sits in
# lib/guardian/topic_guardian.rb, the same directory, so an arm reading the surface reads
# the gold. The slug surface shares no directory with any scoring row.
#
# MEMORIZATION - python3 bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Category
#   {"ok": true, "recall": 0.2, "samples": [0.161, 0.0, 0.2],
#    "reason": "closed-book recall 0.2 < 0.3 - not recited"}
# Not recited. What it did recall is Category's OWN members (ensure_slug, description_text,
# find_by_email, topics_week...); no gold row is a member of Category.
#
# A PROPERTY OF THE SENSE ARM ON THIS ANCHOR, recorded because the next phase will meet it:
# `sense_blast(symbol: "Category")` with no `file` does NOT return a payload. It returns
#     {"ambiguous": true, "matches": 6, "hint": "Multiple symbols named \"Category\" exist -
#      retry this tool with the `file` parameter set to ..."}
# so the arm must make a second call pinning app/models/category.rb before it is shown
# anything. gold_confidence_check reproduces this exactly: run without --file it reported
# "gold rows: 15  shown: 0  missing: 15", and with --file app/models/category.rb it reported
# "gold rows: 15  shown: 15  missing: 0". This is a real cost on the sense arm - one extra
# round trip - and it is also a real recovery path, since the hint names the file to pass.
# It is NOT a defect in the checker; the checker takes --file and I had omitted it.
#
# THE HONEST RISK, stated before the run: the scorer cannot enforce the enclosing-routine
# demand. `bench/lib/gold.py:_cited` credits a gold path plus ANY line number, so an arm
# that dumps grep output and names no routine still scores the row. The bet is behavioural,
# and it is a bet, not a measured effect. Only the judge's map_quality and justification
# see the demand directly. If the run returns baseline at or near 1.00 with a transcript
# showing a spooled grep and no Reads, that is a routed lever for the human, not a kill
# this phase may make.
name: Discourse work session - audit the category contract before a rework
repo: discourse
contract_symbol: Category
contract_file: app/models/category.rb
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that represents one of this forum's
  top-level containers - the thing topics are filed under, that carries its own
  permissions, settings and hierarchy, and that half the product reaches for whenever it
  needs to know where something belongs or who is allowed to see it. The rework will change
  what that class hands back to the code that asks it questions. You will work the whole
  ticket in one session: get your bearings, map how the rest of the system gets hold of one
  of these records when all it has is the human-readable path out of a URL, follow one of
  these records through being brought into existence and taken apart again, audit everywhere
  else in the codebase that would have to change with the class, trace how code satisfies
  itself it is acting on a real one, rank what is at risk, and hand over the map a teammate
  could land the change from. Throughout, an answer that points at a place must say which
  routine that place is inside, and give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight
  to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a file:line with
  no routine named around it does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression
  shipped.
steps:
- name: Orient in the codebase
  prompt: |
    Task: orient yourself. In a few sentences plus file:line references, what is this project, how is the code organized, and where does the class that stands for the container topics are filed under sit - the one the rest of the system keeps having to ask about - along with the shared pieces its behaviour is assembled from? Hand the next agent a map so they can act with small, targeted lookups, not a fresh exploration of the codebase. No Explore agents.
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
    description: Project shape and the class's place in it, with paths
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_search
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_conventions
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Map the category contract
  prompt: 'Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with the one
    part of this class''s job that every incoming request leans on: getting hold of one of these records
    when all the system has is the human-readable path the visitor typed, which may name a parent and
    a child, may be an old path that has since been retired, and may not name one of these at all. Trace
    that path end to end. Show every entry point that turns such a path into one of these records, every
    place that walks the hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another, every place that keeps the
    path in step when the record is renamed, and every place that redirects or refuses because the path
    turned out to point somewhere else. For each one give the file:line, the name of the routine it lives
    in, and one phrase on what it contributes to that path. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework
    would have to preserve for that path to keep working. A place named without the routine it lives in
    does not count, and neither does a routine named without the file:line where it is defined. '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '8'
    required: true
    description: Resolution path traced through the routines that make it up, each located
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Trace how one of these records is built up and taken apart
  prompt: |
    Task: trace the lifecycle. Follow one of these records from the point where the system decides it needs one it does not have yet, through it being brought into existence and later renamed or re-described, and on to the point where it is taken apart again, and say what runs around each of those moments. For every layer give the file:line and the name of the routine it lives in, and mark which parts run inline, in the request or task that triggered them, and which are handed off to run later in the background or on a repeating schedule. Where something is handed off, say what it is handed - the record itself, or something it can be looked up from again - because that is what a rework of the contract has to keep working.
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '4'
    required: true
    description: Lifecycle traced through its routines, inline vs deferred marked
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
  prompt: 'Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first part
    already cost you. Widen back out from that one path to the class as a whole, as a single subject,
    and find EVERY place in the codebase that would have to change with it, so the rework cannot silently
    break one. Be exhaustive and group them by area. Most of what you are looking for is not doing this
    class''s job at all: it is in the middle of some other job entirely, it reaches for this class exactly
    once to settle a question that other job needs answered, and the routine it does that from is named
    after the other job and never after this one. So the answer you owe is not where the dependency sits
    but WHOSE behaviour changes: for every dependent, name the routine the dependency is inside, give
    its file:line, and say in one phrase what that routine would start getting wrong if the class stopped
    handing back what it hands back today. A file:line with no routine named around it does not count,
    a routine you cannot state the consequence for does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression
    shipped. '
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Dependents attributed to the enclosing routine, with the consequence named
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Trace the guards that confirm a real, current record
  prompt: |
    Task: work runs against one of these records all over the codebase, and some of what that work is handed is not one at all, or names one that is no longer there. Trace how the code satisfies itself, before it acts, that what it holds really is one of these and that it really still exists: where an incoming thing is checked for being the right kind of thing at all, where a name or an identifier that resolves to nothing is turned into a refusal at the boundary rather than an error further down, and where a reference that was stored earlier is re-checked against what is actually there before it is trusted. Give the file:line and the enclosing routine for each guard, and say in one phrase what it is protecting the caller from.
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
    description: Guards located at their routines, with what each protects against
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Assess the blast radius of the contract change
  prompt: |
    Task: you are about to change what this class hands back. Assess the full blast radius across everything you have found: which dependents are at risk, which are most likely to break, and what must be re-verified afterwards. Rank by risk and say what drives the ranking - separate the ones that would fail loudly and immediately from the ones that would keep running and quietly return something different, which are the expensive ones. Use file:line throughout and name the routine that carries each risk, not just the file it is in. A missed high-risk dependent is the one that pages someone.
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Dependents ranked by risk at their routines, not relisted
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Produce the change + verification map
  prompt: |
    Task: produce the complete map for landing this safely: the pieces of the contract itself you will edit, every dependent that has to be reviewed or touched grouped by area, and the tests that would have to be updated or added to cover the behaviour that moves. Use file:line and the enclosing routine throughout, so a teammate could land the change from your map alone without re-exploring the codebase.
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: An actionable map - edits, dependents by area, and the test surface
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3
gold:
- id: contract-model
  group: contract
  match:
  - app/models/category.rb
  relation: app/models/category.rb:1083 `def self.find_by_slug_path(slug_path)` - the contract under rework
    and the class-level entry point every slug-path lookup bottoms out in; :1103 `find_by_slug_path_with_id`
    is the trailing-id variant that falls back to it at :1110, :985 `def self.query_category(slug_or_id,
    parent_category_id)` is the one-level lookup the hierarchy walk is built from, :627 `def ensure_slug`
    keeps the stored slug in step across a rename, and :16 `SLUG_REF_SEPARATOR = ":"` is the separator
    the ref form is built from
- id: c:categories-controller
  group: contract
  match:
  - app/controllers/categories_controller.rb
  relation: app/controllers/categories_controller.rb:394 `category = Category.find_by_slug_path(params[:slug_path].split("/"))`
    inside `CategoriesController#find` (def at :387) - the HTTP front door onto the slug-path lookup,
    raising Discourse::NotFound when it comes back blank
- id: c:hierarchical-search
  group: contract
  match:
  - app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb
  relation: app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb:53 `scope = Category.from("allowed_categories").select(:id)`
    inside `Category::Query::HierarchicalSearch#matched_scope` - walks the parent/child hierarchy to disambiguate
    one level from another
- id: c:permalink
  group: contract
  match:
  - app/models/permalink.rb
  relation: 'app/models/permalink.rb:82 `return category.relative_url if category` - the retired-path
    case: an old URL that no longer resolves is redirected through the permalink record''s category association
    declared at line 6'
- id: d:user-destroyer
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/services/user_destroyer.rb
  relation: 'app/services/user_destroyer.rb:37 `category_topic_ids = Category.where.not(topic_id: nil).pluck(:topic_id)`
    inside `UserDestroyer#destroy` - deleting a user has to spare the topics that are category description
    topics; the routine is about destroying a user and never about categories'
- id: d:search-indexer
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/services/search_indexer.rb
  relation: app/services/search_indexer.rb:345 `if Category === obj && (obj.saved_change_to_name? || force)`
    inside `SearchIndexer.index` - the search index dispatches on the record's class to decide it must
    reindex on a rename; the routine is the generic indexing entry point
- id: d:email-receiver
  group: dependents
  match:
  - lib/email/receiver.rb
  relation: lib/email/receiver.rb:849 `return true if destination.is_a?(Category) && destination.mailinglist_mirror?`
    inside `Email::Receiver#sent_to_mailinglist_mirror?` - inbound mail type-tests its destination against
    the class to decide mailing-list mirror handling; the routine is about email delivery
- id: d:topic-view-serializer
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/serializers/topic_view_serializer.rb
  relation: 'app/serializers/topic_view_serializer.rb:212 `return nil if !scope.can_see_category?(Category.find_by(id:
    topic_timer.category_id))` inside `TopicViewSerializer#topic_timer` - the timer attribute is suppressed
    when the viewer cannot see the target category; the routine is about serializing a topic timer'
- id: d:seed-topics
  group: dependents
  match:
  - lib/seed_data/topics.rb
  relation: 'lib/seed_data/topics.rb:63 `general_category = Category.find_by(id: SiteSetting.general_category_id)`
    inside `SeedData::Topics#topics` - new-site seeding resolves three configured categories before it
    can place its seed topics; the routine is about seeding topics'
- id: d:user-notifications
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/mailers/user_notifications.rb
  relation: 'app/mailers/user_notifications.rb:633 `"#{Category.where(id: category.parent_category_id).pick(:name)}/#{show_category_in_subject}"`
    inside `UserNotifications#send_notification_email` - the notification subject line reaches for the
    parent category''s name; the routine is about sending an email'
- id: d:chat-channel-fetcher
  group: dependents
  match:
  - plugins/chat/lib/chat/channel_fetcher.rb
  relation: plugins/chat/lib/chat/channel_fetcher.rb:124 `Category.subcategory_ids(options[:chatable_id].to_i)`
    inside `Chat::ChannelFetcher.secured_public_channel_search` - chat channel search expands a category
    into its subcategories to scope the search; the routine is about fetching chat channels
- id: d:topics-controller
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/controllers/topics_controller.rb
  relation: app/controllers/topics_controller.rb:220 `category = Category.find(params[:destination_category_id])`
    inside `TopicsController#publish` - publishing a shared draft resolves its destination container;
    the routine is about publishing a topic
- id: d:tags-search
  group: dependents
  match:
  - app/services/tags/search.rb
  relation: app/services/tags/search.rb:217 `Category` inside `Tags::Search#explain_exclusion` - tag search
    explains why a tag was excluded by joining back to the category restriction; the routine is about
    explaining a tag exclusion
- id: d:topic-voting
  group: dependents
  match:
  - plugins/discourse-topic-voting/lib/discourse_topic_voting/topic_extension.rb
  relation: plugins/discourse-topic-voting/lib/discourse_topic_voting/topic_extension.rb:18 `SiteSetting.topic_voting_enabled
    && regular? && Category.can_vote?(category_id) &&` inside `DiscourseTopicVoting::TopicExtension#can_vote?`
    - voting eligibility is gated on a class-level predicate about the container; the routine is about
    whether a topic can be voted on
- id: d:migrations-optimizer
  group: dependents
  match:
  - migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb
  relation: migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb:43 `@category_id = Category.last.id` inside `Migrations::Uploader::Tasks::Optimizer#initialize_discourse_resources`
    - the import optimizer needs some category to attach its resources to; the routine is about initialising
    uploader resources
